{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"b81n3euhi","owned_by_subagent":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_014H6xzh92h7XGAuR8yDj1cs","description":"cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-kke16c/translated_rust\nexport CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true\nH=/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-kke16c/tmp/hdiff\nR=$(pwd)\ndeclare -A NV=( [128s]=16 [128f]=16 [192s]=24 [192f]=24 [256s]=32 [256f]=32 )\ndeclare -A FM=( [128s]=21 [128f]=25 [192s]=30 [192f]=33 [256s]=39 [256f]=40 )\ndeclare -A DG=( [128s]=30 [128f]=34 [192s]=39 [192f]=42 [256s]=47 [256f]=49 )\nrc=0\nfor th in robust simple; do\n  for sp in 128s 128f 192s 192f 256s 256f; do\n    cargo build --release --no-default-features --features \"haraka $th $sp\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo \"BUILD FAIL\"; rc=1; continue; }\n    cp target/release/libsphincsplus.so \"$TMPDIR/r-$th-$sp.so\"\n    $H \"$R/cbuild/haraka-$th-$sp/app/libsphincs_core_det.so\" \\\n       \"$R/cbuild/haraka-$th-$sp/lib/haraka/libharaka.so\" \\\n       \"$TMPDIR/r-$th-$sp.so\" ${NV[$sp]} ${FM[$sp]} ${DG[$sp]} \"haraka-$th-$sp\" || rc=1\n  done\ndone\necho \"OVERALL rc=$rc\"","is_backgrounded":false,"task_type":"local_bash","uuid":"5d910d6f-5d97-49f3-a29f-5e12d1268329","session_id":"0a57186a-e772-4c86-931a-f38c37ec34b0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_gnpwyn7rok24by7vavuanxla6mwfwcx2zkxc5ooorhg7zr6genpa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Verdict\n\n**Zero behavioural divergences found in `sha2.c` → `sha2.rs`.** Every function is exactly equivalent. Below is the evidence, then a numbered list of the only non-functional / latent differences (items 1–2 are real code differences worth flagging; 3–7 are documented-as-benign).\n\n## Verification performed\n\nConstant tables diffed mechanically (not by eye):\n- `K256`: extracted all 64 `F_32(wN, 0x…)` immediates from `sha2.c:182-254` in source order and compared to `K_256` (`sha2.rs:130-147`) — **0 mismatches**; both also equal FIPS-180-4 (recomputed from cube roots of the first 64 primes).\n- `K512`: all 80 `F_64(wN,0x…ULL)` immediates from `sha2.c:331-422` vs `K_512` (`sha2.rs:150-171`) — **0 mismatches**; both equal FIPS-180-4.\n- `iv_256` (`sha2.c:459-464`) vs `IV_256` (`sha2.rs:352-357`) — 32/32 bytes identical, equal to the FIPS SHA-256 IV.\n- `iv_512` (`sha2.c:466-473`) vs `IV_512` (`sha2.rs:360-367`) — 64/64 bytes identical, equal to the FIPS SHA-512 IV.\n\nDifferential execution (the strongest evidence): I compiled `c_src/lib/sha2/src/sha2.c` standalone with gcc (`-DPARAMS=sphincs-sha2-{128f,192f,256f}`) and compiled `src/backends/sha2/sha2.rs` unmodified as a `cdylib` via a `#[path]` shim supplying `params`/`context`/`utils`, then compared all 11 exported symbols through ctypes (scratch files in `$TMPDIR/shatest`, nothing in the repo was modified):\n- `sha256`/`sha512` for every length 0–299 plus 400/511/512/513/1000/1024/4096, and 300 random lengths in 0–5000: identical, and both equal `hashlib`.\n- `sha256_inc_init`/`sha512_inc_init`: full 40/72-byte state identical (including the 8 zero counter bytes).\n- `sha256_inc_blocks` + `sha256_inc_finalize` for `inblocks` 0–5 × 22 tail lengths (0,1,2,54,55,56,57,63,64,65,111,112,119,120,127,128,129,200,255,256,257,300), comparing **both** the digest and the full post-call state: identical.\n- `sha512_inc_blocks` + `sha512_inc_finalize` for `inblocks` 0–4 × 19 tail lengths (incl. 110,111,112,113,127,128,129,245,246,247,248,255,256,257): identical.\n- Counter/padding edge cases: hand-crafted 8-byte big-endian counters `0, 1, 0xFF, 2^61, 0x0007FFFFFFFFFFFF, 0x1FFF…FF, 0x2000…00, 0xDEADBEEFCAFEBABE, 2^64-8, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` × 11 lengths, both hashes (220 cases) — identical, so the `bytes >> 53 … bytes << 3` / `bytes >> 61 …` bit-length encodings and 64-bit counter wraparound agree exactly.\n- `SPX_mgf1_256`/`SPX_mgf1_512` for `inlen` ∈ {0,1,4,16,22,24,32,48,64,100} × `outlen` ∈ 0–199 ∪ {256,257,300,1000}, comparing the output buffer **plus 16 guard bytes** past `outlen` (to catch over-writes): identical, including `outlen == 0` and exact-multiple-of-32/64 cases (no tail) and 1-byte tails.\n- `SPX_seed_state` for 500 random `pub_seed`s at SPX_N=16 (SPX_SHA512=0), 300 at N=24, 500 at N=32, comparing the whole C-visible struct: identical, and at N=16 the Rust side provably wrote **nothing** past C's 72-byte struct.\n\nI also independently simulated in Python the C fully-unrolled rolling-register form (`M_32`/`EXPAND_32` in-place over `w0..w15`, 16 rounds per group) against the Rust array form (`w[16..64] = σ1(w[i-2])+w[i-7]+σ0(w[i-15])+w[i-16]`, 64/80 straight rounds) for 147 lengths — bit-identical and equal to `hashlib`, confirming the loop-rolling rewrite is exact.\n\n## Per-function result\n\n- `load_bigendian_32/64` (`sha2.c:13-23` / `sha2.rs:26-43`): identical byte indices and shift amounts (3,2,1,0 → 0,8,16,24; 7…0 → 0,8,…,56). No divergence.\n- `store_bigendian_32/64` (`sha2.c:25-51` / `sha2.rs:46-73`): identical; note the Rust version correctly preserves C's odd `store_bigendian_32(uint8_t*, uint64_t)` signature and only writes 4 bytes, and callers correctly pass `state[i] as u64` (`sha2.rs:250-257`) mirroring C's implicit `uint32_t → uint64_t` zero-extension. No divergence.\n- Round primitives (`sha2.c:53-68` / `sha2.rs:80-127`): all rotate amounts and directions match (Σ0₃₂ 2/13/22, Σ1₃₂ 6/11/25, σ0₃₂ ROTR7/ROTR18/SHR3, σ1₃₂ ROTR17/ROTR19/SHR10, Σ0₆₄ 28/34/39, Σ1₆₄ 14/18/41, σ0₆₄ ROTR1/ROTR8/SHR7, σ1₆₄ ROTR19/ROTR61/SHR6). C's `ROTR(x,c)=(x>>c)|(x<<(w-c))` is used only with `1 ≤ c ≤ w-1`, so `rotate_right` is exact. All `>>` are logical in both. No divergence.\n- `crypto_hashblocks_sha256` (`sha2.c:133-288` / `sha2.rs:175-260`): identical `while (inlen >= 64)`, `in += 64; inlen -= 64`, final feed-forward, byte-order of state load/store, and return of the leftover `inlen`. All additions `wrapping_*`. No divergence.\n- `crypto_hashblocks_sha512` (`sha2.c:290-456` / `sha2.rs:264-349`): same, `>= 128` / `-= 128`, 80 rounds. No divergence (see note 2 on the return type).\n- `sha256_inc_init` / `sha512_inc_init` (`sha2.c:475-491` / `sha2.rs:374-391`): IV into `[0,32)`/`[0,64)`, zeros into `[32,40)`/`[64,72)`. No divergence.\n- `sha256_inc_blocks` / `sha512_inc_blocks` (`sha2.c:493-509` / `sha2.rs:394-411`): counter at offset 32 / 64, loaded before compression, `+= 64*inblocks` / `128*inblocks`, stored big-endian. No divergence (see note 3).\n- `sha256_inc_finalize` (`sha2.c:511-557` / `sha2.rs:414-464`): `bytes = load_be64(state+32) + inlen` (wrapping), `inlen &= 63`, `in -= inlen`, `padded[inlen]=0x80`, `if (inlen < 56)` → zeros `[inlen+1,56)`, length at `56..63`, hash 64 bytes; else zeros `[inlen+1,120)`, length at `120..127`, hash 128 bytes; copy 32 bytes out. All identical including the `<< 3` low byte. No divergence.\n- `sha512_inc_finalize` (`sha2.c:559-606` / `sha2.rs:467-519`): `inlen &= 127`, `if (inlen < 112)` → zeros `[inlen+1,119)`, 9 length bytes at `119..127`, hash 128; else zeros `[inlen+1,247)`, length at `247..255`, hash 256; copy 64 bytes out. Identical. No divergence.\n- `sha256` / `sha512` (`sha2.c:608-620` / `sha2.rs:522-535`): 40 / 72-byte stack state, init then finalize. No divergence.\n- `mgf1_256` / `mgf1_512` (`sha2.c:628-675` / `sha2.rs:547-607`): identical `while (i+1)*OUT <= outlen` loop, `u32_to_bytes(inbuf+inlen, i)` (32→u32 truncation matched), `out += OUT` per iteration, post-loop `i` value, tail `if outlen > i*OUT` with `memcpy` of `outlen - i*OUT`. `SPX_u32_to_bytes` (`src/utils.rs:25-30`) is byte-identical to `u32_to_bytes` (`c_src/app/src/utils.c:24-30`). No divergence.\n- `seed_state` (`sha2.c:683-701` / `sha2.rs:614-631`): 128-byte `block`, `pub_seed[0..SPX_N]` then zeros to 128, `sha256_inc_init`+`sha256_inc_blocks(...,1)` always, `sha512_*` only when `SPX_SHA512`. `SPX_SHA512 = SPX_N >= 24` in `src/params.rs:140` exactly reproduces the headers (128f/s → 0, 192f/s → 1, 256f/s → 1). No divergence.\n- Header constants: `SPX_SHA256_BLOCK_BYTES 64`, `SPX_SHA256_OUTPUT_BYTES 32`, `SPX_SHA512_BLOCK_BYTES 128`, `SPX_SHA512_OUTPUT_BYTES 64` (`sha2.h:7-11` / `sha2.rs:16-19`) — all match. `SPX_SHA256_ADDR_BYTES 22` (`sha2.h:17`) is mirrored at `src/params.rs:157` = 22.\n- Symbol namespacing matches: `sha256*`/`sha512*` un-namespaced, `SPX_mgf1_256`/`SPX_mgf1_512`/`SPX_seed_state` namespaced (`sha2.h:32-41`); `crypto_hashblocks_*` private in both.\n\n## Numbered list of differences (none change observable behaviour)\n\n1. **`spx_ctx` layout: `state_seeded_512` present unconditionally (latent ABI difference, only for 128s/128f).**\n   - C: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-kke16c/translated_rust/c_src/app/include/context.h:16-20` — `state_seeded_512[72]` exists only under `# if SPX_SHA512`.\n   - Rust: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-kke16c/translated_rust/src/context.rs:19-22` — both `state_seeded: [u8;40]` and `state_seeded_512: [u8;72]` are gated only on `#[cfg(feature = \"sha2\")]`.\n   - Measured: SPX_N=16 → C `sizeof(spx_ctx)` = **72**, Rust = **144**. SPX_N=24 → 160/160. SPX_N=32 → 176/176. Field offsets agree in all three cases (`pub_seed`=0, `sk_seed`=SPX_N, `state_seeded`=2·SPX_N).\n   - Behaviour: identical, because `SPX_SHA512` is a `const bool` so `SPX_seed_state` (`sha2.rs:627`) compiles the `sha512_*` calls out; I verified experimentally that at N=16 Rust writes nothing past byte 72.\n   - Exposing input: none through `SPX_seed_state`. It would only matter for a mixed C/Rust link at 128s/128f where one side does `sizeof(spx_ctx)` / `malloc` / `memcpy` / arrays of `spx_ctx` (e.g. a C caller `memcpy(dst, ctx, sizeof(spx_ctx))` after Rust filled it, or Rust allocating a ctx handed to C code that `memset`s `sizeof`). Purely latent.\n\n2. **`crypto_hashblocks_sha512` return type: C `int`, Rust `u64`.**\n   - C: `c_src/lib/sha2/src/sha2.c:290` (`static int … unsigned long long inlen`) and `:455` (`return inlen;` — truncation of a 64-bit value to `int`, implementation-defined above `INT_MAX`).\n   - Rust: `src/backends/sha2/sha2.rs:264-268` (`-> u64`) and `:348` (`inlen`).\n   - Unobservable: both call sites (`sha2.c:505`, `sha2.c:563`; `sha2.rs:407`, `sha2.rs:476`) discard the result. Note the `sha256` counterpart is `size_t`/`usize` and matches exactly (`sha2.c:133`/`:287` vs `sha2.rs:175-179`/`:259`).\n   - Minimal exposing input: none — no input can expose it without changing the call sites.\n\n3. **Non-wrapping Rust arithmetic where C wraps (panics only if `overflow-checks` is enabled).** C's unsigned arithmetic wraps; these Rust sites use plain operators:\n   - `sha2.rs:397` `64 * inblocks` vs `sha2.c:496` (note the inconsistency with `sha2.rs:398`, which *does* use `wrapping_mul` for the same product).\n   - `sha2.rs:563` `(i + 1) * outb` and `sha2.rs:595` vs `sha2.c:638` / `sha2.c:664`.\n   - `sha2.rs:554` / `sha2.rs:586` `inlen_us + 4` vs `sha2.c:631` / `sha2.c:657`.\n   - Exposing input: `inblocks ≥ 2^58`, or `outlen ≥ 2^59`, or `inlen ≥ usize::MAX-3`. Unreachable — real callers pass `inblocks ∈ {1,2}` (`src/backends/sha2/hash_sha2.rs:45,47`) and `outlen` of a few hundred bytes; and the project's release profile has overflow checks off, so even then it would wrap like C.\n\n4. **Loop-rolling rewrite (intentional, proven equivalent).** C uses 64/80 fully-unrolled `F_32`/`F_64` invocations with the round constant as an immediate plus in-place `EXPAND_32`/`EXPAND_64` over 16 rolling registers (`sha2.c:73-107`, `:182-254`, `:331-422`); Rust uses `K_256`/`K_512` tables plus `w: [u32;64]`/`[u64;80]` (`sha2.rs:200-226`, `:289-315`). Verified numerically identical (Python simulation of both forms + the ctypes differential above). No behavioural difference; only a code-shape and constant-storage (immediates vs `.rodata`) difference.\n\n5. **Pre-zeroed buffers vs C's uninitialised ones (no observable difference).** `sha2.rs:420` `let mut padded = [0u8; 128]` vs `sha2.c:512` `uint8_t padded[128]`; `sha2.rs:473` `[0u8; 256]` vs `sha2.c:560`; `sha2.rs:554/586` `vec![0u8; inlen+4]` vs `sha2.c:631/657` `SPX_VLA` (stack VLA, uninitialised). I checked coverage of every byte actually hashed: in each branch the union of `[0,inlen)` (tail copy), `[inlen]` (0x80), the zero-fill range, and the length field covers the full 64/128/256 bytes passed to `crypto_hashblocks_*`, and `inbuf[0..inlen+4)` is fully written before use. So no uninitialised byte is ever read in C, and pre-zeroing cannot mask a difference. Also a stack-VLA→heap-`Vec` change, which is allocation behaviour only.\n\n6. **`inp.add(inlen)` / `inp.sub(inlen)` on a possibly-null pointer (`sha2.rs:424,426` and `:477,479`) vs C's `in += inlen; in -= inlen` (`sha2.c:516,518` and `:564,566`).** Both are UB by the letter of their respective standards when `in == NULL && inlen == 0`; both compile to the same no-op. Not a divergence.\n\n7. **Missing compile-time guard.** `sha2.h:13-15` has `#if SPX_SHA256_OUTPUT_BYTES < SPX_N #error …`; `sha2.rs` has no equivalent `const_assert`. Cannot fire for any shipped parameter set (SPX_N ≤ 32 = `SPX_SHA256_OUTPUT_BYTES`), so no behavioural impact."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2144,"cache_read_input_tokens":100061,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2144,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01XUoVkG7eFJ5qnx2yCXCWnT","session_id":"0a57186a-e772-4c86-931a-f38c37ec34b0","uuid":"a6a0bdbe-248e-41c4-b2a8-2469e33c4f46","timestamp":"2026-08-21T13:30:49.332Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Compare sha2 primitives C vs Rust"}
