{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_n5bgl3ak7lgnalmwq6v2j6ukjd23esfpj3bcqe2xf6y5phan5swq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Verdict: **0 behavioural divergences found.**\n\nI compared every function in both groups line-by-line, mechanically diffed every constant table, and then ran real differential tests (ctypes against the prebuilt C `.so`s in `cbuild/` vs. Python transcriptions of the Rust sources). Everything matches.\n\n---\n\n## Mechanical table diffs (all NUMBERS compared, not sampled)\n\n| Table | C | Rust | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `KeccakF_RoundConstants[24]` | `fips202.c:49-62` | `fips202.rs:36-61` | 24/24 identical |\n| blake256 `cst[16]` (u32) | `blake256.c:41-45` | `blake256.rs:45-48` (`CST`) | 16/16 identical |\n| blake512 `cst[16]` (u64) | `blake512.c:45-50` | `blake512.rs:46-63` (`cst`) | 16/16 identical |\n| blake256 `padding[]` | `blake256.c:47-49` — 64 bytes, only `[0]=0x80` | `blake256.rs:51-55` — `[u8;64]`, `p[0]=0x80` | identical |\n| blake512 `padding[129]` | `blake512.c:52-56` — 129 bytes, only `[0]=0x80` | `blake512.rs:66-70` — `[u8;129]`, `p[0]=0x80` | identical |\n| BLAKE message-permutation (the 14/16 `ROUND(...)` arg lists = sigma × cst-index pairing) | `blake256.c:241-254`, `blake512.c:249-264` | `blake256.rs:243-256`, `blake512.rs:272-287` | all 14 and all 16 arg lists byte-identical after whitespace normalisation |\n| BLAKE `ROUND` macro **body** | `blake256.c:127-239`, `blake512.c:135-247` | `blake256.rs:123-241`, `blake512.rs:152-269` | **112 ops each, identical op-for-op** (same operand, same order, same rotation amount) |\n\nBLAKE `ROT` direction: C `((x)<<(w-n))|((x)>>(n))`; Rust `blake256.rs:60` `(x << (32-n)) | (x >> n)`, `blake512.rs:75` `(x << (64-n)) | (x >> n)`. Same expression, same direction. Amounts used: 256 → 16/12/8/7; 512 → 32/25/16/11 — matched positionally by the op-for-op diff. Round counts: 14 (256) and 16 (512), matching C.\n\n---\n\n## Differential test results (C `.so` vs. Rust-transcribed model)\n\n- **Keccak-f[1600]**: the Rust uses a *structurally different* formulation (compact loop with `ROTC`/`PILN` tables, one round per iteration) vs. the C's fully-unrolled two-rounds-per-iteration form. I transcribed **both** and compared over the all-zero state + 200 random 25-lane states: **all 25 lanes identical every time**. Official KATs confirm: `SHA3-256(\"\") = a7ffc6f8…434a`, `SHAKE256(\"\",32) = 46b9dd2b…762f`.\n- **`shake256` one-shot**: identical over `inlen ∈ 0..300` ∪ {135,136,137,271,272,273,400} × `outlen ∈ {1,16,24,32,64,135,136,137,200,272,273,500}` (exercises rate boundary 136, multi-block, tail).\n- **`shake256_inc_*`**: identical over 400 random multi-absorb patterns (1-4 chunks, sizes crossing 136) + 200 patterns with **multi-call incremental squeeze** (exercises the `s_inc[25]` leftover-byte path).\n- **`blake256` / `blake512`**: identical over 221 message lengths (`0..210` plus 254-257, 511-513, 1000, 1023-1025) — this covers all three `final` padding branches: `buflen < 440/888`, `buflen == 440/888` (len 55 / len 111), and `buflen > 440/888` (two-compression branch), plus `buflen == 0` → `nullt=1`. C reference values confirmed against official vectors: `blake256(\"\")=716f6e86…ea7a`, `blake512(\"\")=a8cfbbd7…f5b8`.\n- **`SPX_blake256_mgf1` / `SPX_blake512_mgf1`**: identical over `inlen ∈ {0,1,15,16,17,31,32,48,55,56,63,64,65,127,128,129}` × `outlen ∈ {0,1,16,31,32,33,63,64,65,96,127,128,200}` (exercises exact-multiple and partial-tail).\n- **SPX layer (`SPX_prf_addr`, `SPX_thash`, `SPX_gen_message_random`, `SPX_hash_message`)** for the SHAKE backend: identical across **all 12** `shake-{robust,simple}-{128s,128f,192s,192f,256s,256f}` configurations, 60 random trials each, including the `tree`/`leaf_idx` masks.\n\n---\n\n## Per-function verification notes\n\n**`fips202.c` → `fips202.rs`** — all match:\n- `load64`/`store64` (`fips202.c:25,42` → `.rs:21,30`): identical little-endian loops.\n- `KeccakF1600_StatePermute` (`:71` → `:71`): rewritten but numerically proven equivalent (above). Rust slices exactly 25 lanes, so `s_inc[25]` is never clobbered — same as C, which only touches `state[0..24]`.\n- `keccak_absorb` (`:347` → `:116`): `t[200]`, zero-fill `0..r`, copy `mlen`, `t[mlen] = p` (C's dangling `i` after the loop == `mlen` — correctly rendered as the literal index `t[mlen]`), `t[r-1] |= 128`, `r/8` lanes. Identical.\n- `keccak_squeezeblocks` (`:393` → `:146`): identical, `r>>3` lanes.\n- `keccak_inc_init` (`:415` → `:158`): 26 lanes zeroed.\n- `keccak_inc_absorb` (`:438` → `:165`): identical. Rust hoists `pos = s_inc[25]` before the inner loop; the C re-reads it but the body only XORs into lanes 0..16 (`(r-1)>>3 = 16` for r=136), so `s_inc[25]` cannot alias — semantically identical.\n- `keccak_inc_finalize` (`:475` → `:188`): identical, including `p` at `s_inc[25]` and `128` at byte `r-1`.\n- `keccak_inc_squeeze` (`:496` → `:195`): identical, including the leftover-byte prologue, `h += i; outlen -= i; s_inc[25] -= i`, and `s_inc[25] = r - i` in the block loop.\n- Wrappers `shake256_inc_{init,absorb,finalize,squeeze}`, `shake256_absorb`, `shake256_squeezeblocks`, `shake256` (`:523-598` → `:256-324`): identical; **rate 136**, domain byte **0x1F**, pad byte **0x80**, incremental state = **26 × u64 (200-byte state + 8-byte position at index 25)**.\n\n**`hash_shake.c` → `hash_shake.rs`** — all match:\n- `initialize_hash_function` (`:13` → `:12`): no-op.\n- `prf_addr` (`:21` → `:16`): buffer `[pub_seed(N) ‖ addr(32) ‖ sk_seed(N)]`, `shake256(out, N, buf, 2N+32)`. Identical order and lengths.\n- `gen_message_random` (`:37` → `:32`): absorb `sk_prf(N)`, `optrand(N)`, `m(mlen)`; finalize; squeeze `N`. Identical.\n- `hash_message` (`:58` → `:52`): absorb `R(N)`, `pk(2N)`, `m(mlen)`; squeeze `SPX_DGST_BYTES`; digest = first `SPX_FORS_MSG_BYTES`; `tree = bytes_to_ull(bufp, TREE_BYTES) & (~0u64 >> (64-TREE_BITS))`; `leaf_idx = (u32)bytes_to_ull(bufp, LEAF_BYTES) & (~0u32 >> (32-LEAF_BITS))`. **Masks and pointer advances identical.** All six `SPX_TREE_BITS`/`SPX_LEAF_BITS` values (54/63/54/63/56/64 and 9/3/9/3/8/4) verified; note 256f gives `TREE_BITS == 64` → shift by 0 in both languages (fine).\n\n**`thash_shake_simple.c` / `thash_shake_robust.c` → `thash_shake.rs`** — both match:\n- simple (`:14` → `.rs:43`): `buf = [pub_seed ‖ addr ‖ in]`, one `shake256(out, N, buf, N+32+inblocks*N)`.\n- robust (`:14` → `.rs:12`): bitmask `shake256(bitmask, inblocks*N, buf, N+32)` — absorbing **only** the first `N+32` bytes (the uninitialised tail of the C VLA is correctly *not* absorbed); XOR loop `buf[N+32+i] = in[i] ^ bitmask[i]`; final `shake256(out, N, buf, N+32+inblocks*N)`. Identical.\n\n**`blake256.c` → `blake256.rs`** — all match: `U8TO32`/`U32TO8` (big-endian) ✓; `blake256_compress` ✓ (incl. `v8..v11 = s[i]^cst[i]`, `v12..v15 = cst[4..7]`, `nullt==0` ⇒ `t[0],t[0],t[1],t[1]` XOR, the final `v_i ^= v_{i+8}` then `^= s[i%4]` then `h_i ^=`); `blake256_init` ✓; `blake256_update` ✓; `blake256_final` ✓; `blake256_mgf1` ✓; `blake256` ✓ (`inlen*8`).\n\n**`blake512.c` → `blake512.rs`** — all match, same items. Note the Rust **faithfully reproduces** the upstream asymmetry that `blake512_update` has **no** `if (t[0]==0) t[1]++` carry (`blake512.c:316,324` → `.rs:352,360`) while `blake256_update` **does** (`blake256.c:308,317` → `.rs:322-324,333-335`), and that `blake512_update`'s tail memcpy masks with `& 0x7F` (`:331` → `.rs:370`) while `blake256_update`'s does **not** (`:324` → `.rs:345`).\n\n**Counter/padding arithmetic** (checked expression-by-expression, all 7 in each `update` + all in each `final`): every `>>3`, `<<3`, `& 0x3F`/`& 0x7F`, `>= 512`/`>= 1024`, `t[0] ± {8,64,128,440,512,888,1024}`, `lo = t[0]+buflen` with `if (lo < buflen) hi++`, `msglen` big-endian `hi‖lo` (8 bytes for 256, 16 for 512), the `padding+1` offset, and the `nullt=1` placement (inside `<440/<888` when `buflen==0`; **after** the two updates in the two-compression branch) are 1:1. C's implicit integer conversions (`int` → `u32`/`u64` in mixed comparisons/subtractions) are reproduced exactly by Rust's `as u32`/`as u64` bit-reinterpreting casts, including for hypothetical negative `fill`.\n\n**Struct layout** (`blake.h:13-25` → `.rs` `#[repr(C)]`): C `blakestate256` size 128, offsets h=0 s=32 t=48 buflen=56 nullt=60 buf=64; `blakestate512` size 248, offsets h=0 s=64 t=96 buflen=112 nullt=116 buf=120 — verified by compiling the C declarations. Rust `repr(C)` produces exactly these.\n\n**Params** (`params-sphincs-{shake,blake}-*.h` vs `src/params.rs`): `SPX_N`, `SPX_FULL_HEIGHT`, `SPX_D`, `SPX_FORS_HEIGHT`, `SPX_FORS_TREES` correct for all 12 headers; `SPX_BLAKE512 = (SPX_N >= 24)` matches the `0/1/1` values in the blake headers; `SPX_ADDR_BYTES=32`, `SPX_WOTS_W=16` ✓.\n\n---\n\n## Observations (not divergences — no input can expose them)\n\n1. **`fips202.rs` defines functions the C file does not.** `fips202.h` *declares* `shake128*`, `sha3_256*`, `sha3_512*` but `fips202.c` never defines them, and nothing in `c_src/` calls them (verified by grep). `fips202.rs:224-252, 287-304, 328-392` implements all of them as `#[no_mangle] extern \"C\"`. This makes the Rust cdylib export symbols the C `libshake.so` does not. They are dead code in both builds; the implementations do look correct (standard PQClean form: rate 168 / 136 / 72, domain byte 0x06 for SHA-3), but they are untested by any KAT.\n2. **The Keccak permutation was rewritten, not transcribed.** `fips202.rs:71-113` is a compact table-driven loop; `fips202.c:71-332` is fully unrolled. Proven numerically equivalent here, but it is the one place in these files where equivalence is not visible by inspection.\n3. **Debug-profile panic sites where C would wrap/UB** (all unreachable with real inputs, and `[profile.release] opt-level=3` has overflow checks off): `fips202.rs:17` `a >> (64-offset)` and `blake*.rs` `rot`'s `x << (w-n)` panic if `n==0` (never — `ROTC` has no 0; BLAKE uses 7/8/11/12/16/25/32); `fips202.rs:167` `mlen + s_inc[25]` and `:169` `r - pos` (invariant `s_inc[25] < r` holds); `fips202.rs:138` `t[mlen]` is bounds-checked (`mlen < r ≤ 168 < 200`).\n4. **`blake512.rs:45-46` exports `cst` with `#[no_mangle]`**, matching C's external-linkage `const u64 cst[16]` in `blake512.c:45` (and `blake256.c`'s `cst` is correctly kept `static`/private as `CST`). Symbol parity, no behavioural effect.\n5. `blake.h:9-11`'s `#error` guard for `SPX_BLAKE256_OUTPUT_BYTES < SPX_N` has no Rust counterpart; it can never fire (32 ≥ 32).\n6. C's `blakestate256 S;` / `blakestate512 S;` are uninitialised automatics whose `buf` is never read before being written (`compress(S, S->buf)` is only reached from the branch that fills `buf[left..blocksz]`, with `buf[0..left]` written by prior calls). Rust's `new_zeroed()` is therefore equivalent, not merely \"safer\".\n\n**Scratch files** written only to `$TMPDIR` (`blake_diff.py`, `shake_diff.py`, `spx_shake_diff.py`, `st.c`). No repository file was modified. Note: `Cargo.toml` gained a `[dev-dependencies] libloading` entry on disk during this session — not from me."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3915,"cache_read_input_tokens":170588,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3915,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01V1X68qp4eT95TXi73gkWBs","session_id":"0a57186a-e772-4c86-931a-f38c37ec34b0","uuid":"f002713a-f4e6-4dea-aeca-9f0124cd3949","timestamp":"2026-08-21T13:33:50.065Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Compare shake and blake C vs Rust"}
