{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nwg53vdkznba5r4gz2verysvxnzmwc24l63smv6ekyrijonqk4kq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Verdict: no behavioural divergences found\n\nI audited every function in the four C files against the four Rust files, mechanically diffed all constant tables and every gate/mask/shift expression, verified struct layout and derived parameters numerically for all six `SPX_N`/param sets, and ran a function-level differential test of the C `libharaka.so` against the Rust cdylib. **Zero divergences.**\n\n### DIVERGENCES: none\n\nThe numbered list is empty. Below is the evidence, then non-behavioural observations.\n\n### Mechanical constant/expression diffs\n\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `haraka512_rc64[10][8]` — all 80 `u64` values | **byte-for-byte identical** (`haraka.c:42-51` vs `haraka.rs:15-24`) |\n| Bit-sliced S-box circuit — all 131 gate statements | **identical** after whitespace/`~`→`!` normalisation. C's `br_aes_ct64_bitslice_Sbox` (`haraka.c:87-259`) and `br_aes_ct_bitslice_Sbox` (`haraka.c:261-434`) are identical to each other and to the single Rust macro `def_bitslice_sbox!` (`haraka.rs:78-226`), instantiated for `u64`/`u32`. `~` on `uint32_t`/`uint64_t` is width-preserving in C (no promotion past `unsigned int`), matching Rust `!`. |\n| `br_aes_ct_ortho` / `br_aes_ct64_ortho` — 12 SWAP call sites, masks `0x5555…/0xAAAA…/1`, `0x3333…/0xCCCC…/2`, `0x0F0F…/0xF0F0…/4`, index pairs `(0,1)(2,3)(4,5)(6,7)`, `(0,2)(1,3)(4,6)(5,7)`, `(0,4)(1,5)(2,6)(3,7)` | identical |\n| `shift_rows32` / `shift_rows` (64) — all 7 mask/shift terms each | identical |\n| `mix_columns32` / `mix_columns` — 24 statements, `rotr16`/`rotr32`, exact XOR order incl. the `q7^r7` extra terms on rows 0,1,3,4 | identical |\n| `br_aes_ct64_interleave_in` / `_out` — mask sequence `0x0000FFFF0000FFFF`, `0x00FF00FF00FF00FF`, shifts 16/8, final `x0\\|(x2<<8)` ordering | identical |\n| `haraka512_perm` mix-state — all 13 mask/shift terms (`0x0001…<<5`, `0x0002…<<12`, `0x0004…>>1`, `0x0008…<<6`, `0x0020…<<9`, `0x0040…>>4`, `0x0080…<<3`, `0x2100…>>5`, `0x0210…<<2`, `0x0800…<<4`, `0x1000…>>12`, `0x4000…>>10`, `0x8400…>>3`) | identical |\n| `haraka256` mix-state — all 7 terms (`0x81818181` unshifted, `0x02020202<<1`, `0x04040404<<2`, `0x08080808<<3`, `0x10101010>>3`, `0x20202020>>2`, `0x40404040>>1`) | identical |\n\nNote: there is no AES S-box **lookup table** in either implementation — both use the Boyar–Peralta boolean circuit, verified gate-by-gate above.\n\n### Function-by-function confirmation\n\n- `tweak_constants` (`haraka.c:701` / `haraka.rs:481`) — base-constant copy happens **before** the `haraka_S(buf, 640, pub_seed, SPX_N, ctx)` call in both (so the copy is load-bearing, not dead); loop `i in 0..10`, `interleave_constant32(tweaked256_rc32[i], buf+32*i)` then `interleave_constant(tweaked512_rc64[i], buf+64*i)`, same order. C's byte-wise `memcpy` of 640 bytes ≡ Rust's whole-array value assignment.\n- `haraka_S_absorb` (`haraka.c:717` / `haraka.rs:506`) — rate 32, padding `t[mlen] = 0x1F` (the C post-loop `i == mlen` idiom is reproduced exactly with an explicit counter), `t[r-1] |= 128`.\n- `haraka_S_inc_finalize` (`haraka.c:794` / `haraka.rs:601`) — `s_inc[s_inc[64]] ^= 0x1F`, `s_inc[31] ^= 128`. The asymmetry vs `_absorb` (`^=` here, `|=`-into-zeroed-temp there) is preserved.\n- `haraka_S_inc_absorb` (`haraka.c:769` / `haraka.rs:568`) — Rust hoists `HARAKAS_RATE - s_inc[64]` into `avail`; safe because `s_inc[64]` cannot be aliased by the loop's writes (indices stay `< 32`). `m` advance uses the pre-zeroed `s_inc[64]` in both.\n- `haraka_S_inc_squeeze` (`haraka.c:803` / `haraka.rs:611`) — leftover-consumption offset `32 - s_inc[64] + i`, then block squeezes with `s_inc[64] = 32 - i`.\n- `haraka_S` (`haraka.c:831` / `haraka.rs:648`) — `outlen/32` full blocks then the `outlen % 32` tail via the 32-byte `d` buffer.\n- `haraka512_perm` — 5×2 AES rounds, `tweaked512_rc64[2*i+j]`, mix-state after each pair of rounds.\n- `haraka512` — feed-forward over all 64 bytes, truncation `buf[8..16] → out[0..8]`, `buf[24..32] → out[8..16]`, `buf[32..40] → out[16..24]`, `buf[48..56] → out[24..32]`. Identical (the Rust `copy_nonoverlapping(src, dst, n)` argument order is reversed vs `memcpy(dst, src, n)` — checked, correct).\n- `haraka256` — no truncation, full 32-byte output XORed with input.\n- `initialize_hash_function`, `prf_addr`, `gen_message_random`, `hash_message` (`hash_haraka.c` / `hash_haraka.rs`) — identical buffer construction (`addr[32] ‖ sk_seed[SPX_N]` in a zeroed 64-byte buffer), identical absorb order (`R`, `pk + SPX_N`, `m`), identical `bytes_to_ull` + masking of `tree`/`leaf_idx`. `SPX_bytes_to_ull` in `src/utils.rs:34` matches `c_src/app/src/utils.c:35`.\n- `thash` simple (`thash_haraka_simple.c` / `thash_haraka.rs:69`) — `inblocks==1` → `haraka512`, else → `haraka_S`. Identical.\n- `thash` robust (`thash_haraka_robust.c` / `thash_haraka.rs:15`) — `inblocks==1` → `haraka256(addr)` bitmask + `haraka512`, else → `haraka_S(bitmask, inblocks*SPX_N, addr, 32)` + `haraka_S(out, SPX_N, …)`. Identical.\n\n### Struct layout & parameters (verified with the actual compilers, not by eye)\n\n`spx_ctx` (C `gcc offsetof/sizeof`) vs `SpxCtx` (Rust `size_of/align_of` + real field addresses) — **exact match for all six param sets**:\n\n| | 128s/128f | 192s/192f | 256s/256f |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `sizeof` | 992 | 1008 | 1024 |\n| `pub_seed` / `sk_seed` | 0 / 16 | 0 / 24 | 0 / 32 |\n| `tweaked512_rc64` (640 B) | 32 | 48 | 64 |\n| `tweaked256_rc32` (320 B) | 672 | 688 | 704 |\n| align | 8 | 8 | 8 |\n\n`SPX_N`, `SPX_FULL_HEIGHT`, `SPX_D`, `SPX_FORS_HEIGHT/TREES`, `SPX_ADDR_BYTES`, `SPX_FORS_MSG_BYTES`, `SPX_TREE_BITS/BYTES`, `SPX_LEAF_BITS/BYTES`, `SPX_DGST_BYTES` all match for all six sets. `haraka_offsets.h` (LAYER 3, TREE 8, TYPE 19, KP_ADDR 20, CHAIN_ADDR 27, HASH_ADDR 31, TREE_HGT 27, TREE_INDEX 28) matches `src/params.rs:147-154`.\n\n### Differential execution testing (C `.so` vs Rust `.so`, same process)\n\n`~622,000` byte-exact comparisons, **0 failures**, across all 12 `(robust|simple) × (128s|128f|192s|192f|256s|256f)` configs:\n- `tweak_constants`: full 640-byte + 320-byte tables, 6 random seeds per config\n- `haraka512_perm`, `haraka512`, `haraka256`: random inputs\n- `haraka_S`: **exhaustive** `inlen 0..97 × outlen 0..97` plus `outlen = 640`, with output-overrun guards\n- incremental sponge: **exhaustive** 2-fragment absorb `a,b ∈ 0..70` (spans the 32-byte rate boundary), exhaustive squeeze chunk sizes `1..70`, plus randomized 6-fragment sequences — 65-byte state compared after *every* call\n- `prf_addr`, `gen_message_random`, `hash_message` (digest + `tree` + `leaf_idx`), `thash` for `inblocks 0..80`\n\n**Negative controls** (C robust-128s vs Rust simple-128s → 240 failures; C simple-192s vs Rust simple-192f → 144 failures) confirm the harness is sensitive and the passes are real, not a symbol-resolution artefact.\n\n**End-to-end**: all 12 haraka KAT digests match `expected_kat.txt` (captured from the C reference). Clean build, no warnings.\n\n### Non-behavioural observations (not divergences)\n\n1. `SPX_VLA` (a real C stack VLA) becomes `vec![]` (heap) in `haraka_S_absorb` (`haraka.rs:516`) and both `thash` variants (`thash_haraka.rs:23-24`, `:77`). Output-identical; differs only in allocation strategy/stack usage. For `inblocks == 0`, C forms a zero-length VLA (ISO C UB, GCC extension) while Rust's empty `Vec` is well-defined — tested identical.\n2. Rust zero-initialises `outbuf`, `d`, `buf_tmp` where C leaves them uninitialised (C fully overwrites before reading in every path). Strictly safer, same output.\n3. `src/backends/haraka/mod.rs` re-exports only `haraka_S_inc_*` and `tweak_constants` at the Rust module level, whereas `haraka.h` also declares `haraka_S`, `haraka512_perm`, `haraka512`, `haraka256` public. They *are* exported from the cdylib via `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` (confirmed with `nm -D`), so the C ABI surface is identical; only Rust-level visibility differs.\n4. **Out of scope but flagged since context.h was in scope**: `src/context.rs:19` includes `state_seeded_512: [u8; 72]` unconditionally under `feature = \"sha2\"`, while `c_src/app/include/context.h:16-19` guards it with `#if SPX_SHA512`, which is `0` for `sha2-128s`/`sha2-128f`. So for the **SHA2** backend at 128s/128f, C `sizeof(spx_ctx) == 72` but Rust `== 144`. The Rust comment acknowledges this (\"always present to keep the layout simple\"). It is self-consistent within Rust and has **no effect on the haraka backend**, but it would matter if C and Rust `spx_ctx` objects were ever exchanged.\n5. `context.rs` gates the haraka fields on `not(sha2) && not(shake) && not(blake)` rather than a positive `haraka` feature. This matches the `lib.rs` backend priority and C's `#ifdef SPX_HARAKA`, but a nonsensical combination like `--features \"haraka sha2\"` would drop them (consistently with backend selection).\n6. Latent, unreachable in both: `hash_message`'s `else` branch computes `>> (64 - SPX_TREE_BITS)`, which would be a `>> 64` for `SPX_D == 1` (UB in C, overflow in Rust). The `if (SPX_D == 1)` guard makes it dead at runtime in both, and no param set has `SPX_D == 1`.\n\nScratch artefacts live in `$TMPDIR` (`hdiff.c`, `hdiff2.c`); no files in the repo were modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2861,"cache_read_input_tokens":154879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2861,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01GdRdw95fm4JWe5RPT853Hb","session_id":"0a57186a-e772-4c86-931a-f38c37ec34b0","uuid":"7e96f502-409d-468f-8c83-a882fc6f01c2","timestamp":"2026-08-21T13:35:56.219Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Compare haraka backend C vs Rust"}
